A||N||13||FieroJoe||Sunroof swap urgent question: Can't get panel flush!||||4||
Z||000000||FieroJoe||06-28-2001||09:02 PM||||I started on my sunroof swap today, took out inteior parts, finally got hard top off - blah blah and all that.<P>So, problem time.  I can't get the new sunroof panel to sit flush into the groove by the windsheild.  It does not want to go down at all, I have pushed very hard.  The A-Pillars are the worse, the leading edge of the rood seems to be a little better.  But, the only thing that I can think of being the culprit here is the strips of butyl rubber that held the old roof on.  Should I take those off the car first, put them on the panel, and then try to fit the roof?<P>That's the only thing that I can think of.  When I took the old roof off, most of the goo stayed on the car, so that's where I left it.<P>Here's some pics to illustrate.<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/P6280018_copy.jpg"> <P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/P6280020_copy.jpg"> <P>Please help ASAP!!!  I need to get the roof on so I can drive, my only car right now!!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif"><P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/joesig_1.jpg"> <BR>'87 Coupe 5spd.<BR>AIM:FieroJoe87||||reg||4
Z||000001||84Bill||06-28-2001||10:01 PM||||Can you adjust your fender?||||reg||5
Z||000002||FieroJoe||06-28-2001||10:02 PM||||Update!<P>I tried out the windsheild molding strip.  I assume that this also has a big role in holding down the roof section.  Though I cannot get the panel down as far as the old one was, if I install the molding I think I can be happy with how it will look.<P>One major problem here though is that the gap at the top of the roof panel and the windshield is quite large.  The molding just sits in there, it dosent press in.  Should I put in the bolts at the bottom, by the fenders and below the windsheild, to pull the roof panel forward?  What steps should I follow when putting the molding back in?  I assume that it needs to be installed with some weatherstrip adhesive, or something like that.  I think that if I use enough of the weatherstrip adhesive, I can get the molding to stay in there, and not leak.  Leaking into my interior is my biggest fear right now.<P>Any other suggestions are very very welcome!||||reg||1
Z||000003||FieroJoe||06-28-2001||10:04 PM||||No, the fender is not the problem, it is unbolted right now and just hanging there.  One thing I forgot in my last post is that I have removed the butyl rubber from the car and installed it on the sunroof panel, but it did not help very much.||||reg||1
Z||000004||mbramble||06-28-2001||10:10 PM||||Is everything lined up properly -- the studs and bolt holes in the top, the holes down at the part below the windshield??  You should have "about" 3/8" or so gap between the edge of the windshield and the edge of the sunroof on the sides and at the top.  This is where the trim piece fits in.<P>If so, the only thing that I can think is that you had some "goo" remain on the panel from the original car and you are now trying to put it on with 2 layers -- that left on the panel and that left on your car.<P>The trim strip is just that, trim.  It doesn't keep water from leaking in.  That is done by proper installation of the windshield itself.  You could even leave the trim out -- it would just look ugly.||||reg||1
Z||000005||FieroJoe||06-28-2001||10:27 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by mbramble:<BR>Is everything lined up properly -- the studs and bolt holes in the top, the holes down at the part below the windshield?? <HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Yes, I have even tightend down two nuts and two bolts just to see if it would move it much.<P><BR> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>You should have "about" 3/8" or so gap between the edge of the windshield and the edge of the sunroof on the sides and at the top.  This is where the trim piece fits in.<BR><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Yeah, I've got that on the A pillars, but the leading edge of the roof seems like it is really wide compared to what my old one looked like<P> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>If so, the only thing that I can think is that you had some "goo" remain on the panel from the original car and you are now trying to put it on with 2 layers -- that left on the panel and that left on your car.<BR><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Nope, made sure of that, the next thing I will try though is thinning out what is there now, a lot!  How important is it?  Is it stuff for water tightness?  Or just crap to hold the panel down?<P> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR><BR>The trim strip is just that, trim.  It doesn't keep water from leaking in.  That is done by proper installation of the windshield itself.  You could even leave the trim out -- it would just look ugly.<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Well that is a relief to me.  But the molding sure does help keep those A Pillar parts down tight against the metal.  I guess the main thing now is how important is the black crap?  Can I substitute something different, and thinner that would work ok?<P>||||reg||1
Z||000006||84Bill||06-28-2001||10:28 PM||||Look at the holes in the cowl where the 10mm bolts go through and line them up. The tsurface of the windshield should be close to flush with the bevel on the roof. I had problems with min since the windshueld was replace and it sits to low in the frame and the trim is all cattywompass. The gap should be about 1/4 of an inch all the way around the entire windshield. <BR>The goopy stuff is to keep the roof skin pillers from moving around not for sealing the roof.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Master_Sushi||06-28-2001||10:42 PM||||This somewhat happend to me when i put a sunroof in a few weeks ago. I had a bit of a gap but not that much. I did a few things to fix it. I first put a big heavy block on t he roof in the area it was sticking up over night. This made it a bit better. Also I had my windshield replace while swaping the roofs (I broke it), they put a new bigger windshield trim on while replaceing the windshield. This made it unnoticeable. But after the car has sat outside for a while in the hot sun it seems to have settled now. I pretty sure it was obviously to much of the stupid goop that was my problum. Anyway hope this helps somewhat. I will post some pics of my car once I get a digital cam. Its really changed allot since I last put up pics of it(if anyone remembers it :P).||||reg||1
Z||000008||FieroJoe||06-28-2001||10:57 PM||||Well, here is my plan for tomorrow, I am done messing with it tonight!<P>I will take the roof off again, and take out a bunch of the butyl rubber crap.  Maybe all of it.  I will then use some silicone caulk in its place, at least along the leading edge of the windsheild.  I will do this there because I fear water getting by the windsheild trim, and blowing underneath the roof area and then soaking my headliner.  I know this is very unlikely, but I don't want to take the chance.<P>I will then bolt down all the bolts on the panel, and see how it lines up.<P>If it looks good, I will lay a bead of weatherstrip adhesive in the gap between the roof skin and the windshield, and then press in my molding as best I can.<P>Also, I believe my windsheild has been replaced before, becuase my molding looks wider than the stuff I saw on FieroMaster88's coupe on monday.  I even have another molding from the car that I pulled the sunroof skin off of, and the splined part is even wider, so that might help too.<P>Anyways, thank you all for the suggestions.  Hopefully, I can get it looking good and all bolted down tomorrow and then reassembled.<P>The next big obstacle to conquer is cutting my headliner to fit, but I have a few ideas on that too so I think I should be alright.||||reg||1
Z||000009||mbramble||06-28-2001||11:11 PM||||I put the solid headliner in the car (with the old fabric removed), installed the rear clothes hangers to hold it in place and then my son held the front in place.  I then used a utility knife and cut the hole out from the top.  Removed it all, installed the new fabric and re-installed it in the car.  Make sure to leave lots of extra cloth when you cut out the hole because you will need it to fit up into the trim ring that goes around the sunroof opening.||||reg||1
Z||000010||FieroJoe||06-28-2001||11:14 PM||||Exactly my plan for the headliner!  Great minds must think alike  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>Anyways, my headliner was just redone last year, so it is all in good shape.  I will be cutting through both the backer-board and the cloth.  I did see how it is tucked into that channel on the car I pulled all my parts from, it is very similar to how windows are screened, with the spline holding the screen in to the frame.||||reg||1
Z||000011||84Bill||06-29-2001||12:02 AM||||If you ever get your windshield replaced. MAKE SURE the use spacers between the frame and the windshield!. It's about 1/4 inch high. If they dont get installd your windshield will sit to low in the frame.<BR> <IMG SRC="http://cn308598-a.wall1.pa.home.com/fiero/op/spacer.jpg"> <BR>||||reg||4
Z||000012||Galen||06-29-2001||12:24 AM||||I didn't see it, but why use the old goo? just replace it?<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.webjolt.f2s.com/pictures/kid.gif">         <BR>1988 Hawaiin Orchid Coupe  <BR>AIM: GalenRPG||||reg||1
Z||000013||FieroJoe||06-29-2001||09:21 AM||||Yup, I've got two of those, they were in my old roof at the bottom of the windsheild.<P>I just got up and now I'm going to go see what I can do with the roof.  Hopefully I can get something working!||||reg||1
